<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian II Emanuel, Elector of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1662">1662</a>–<a href="/years/1726">1726</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Max Emanuel · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Emanuel was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1679 to 1726. He won fame fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, storming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1688, and governed the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from 1692. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, lost Bavaria after the defeat at Blenheim in 1704 and went into exile, regaining his lands only in 1714.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-emanuel-elector-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Maximilian II Emanuel, Elector of Bavaria" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas Møller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%B6ller_-_Maximilian_II_Emanuel_of_Bavaria_-_Torre_Abbey.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maximilian II Emanuel was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 11 July 1662, the son of Ferdinand Maria, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and Princess Henriette Adelaide of Savoy. He inherited the electorate while still a minor in 1679 and remained under the regency of his uncle Maximilian Philipp until 1680. By 1683 he had begun a military career, fighting in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> against the Ottoman attempt to push further into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. On 15 July 1685 he married Maria Antonia, daughter of Emperor Leopold I, an unhappy marriage that nonetheless produced an heir for both Bavaria and the Spanish monarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His fame was assured in 1688 when he led the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> from the Turks, with the support of Serbian insurgents under Jovan Monasterlija. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">War of the Grand Alliance</a> he fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>' side on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> frontier and was made governor of the Spanish Netherlands in late 1691. His son Joseph Ferdinand, born in October 1692, was named heir to the Spanish crown but died in 1699. In 1695 French troops besieged and bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, and the resulting fire was the most destructive event in the city's history.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the defeat at Blenheim in 1704 the Bavarian court went to the Spanish Netherlands, his sons were held for years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was partitioned between Austria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, whose harsh administration provoked a serious peasant uprising. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Battle of Ramillies</a> on 23 May 1706 he found refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rastatt">Treaty of Rastatt</a> in 1714 compelled Austria to restore him fully, including the Upper Palatinate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in Bavaria Max Emanuel turned to building to make up for the failure of his political ambitions, and was bitter that others were raised to royal rank. In 1717 he sent troops against the Turks again, and in 1724 he united the Wittelsbach lines, which with his son Clemens August of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and the electors Palatine and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> commanded four votes in an imperial election. He died of a stroke in 1726 and is buried in the crypt of the Theatinerkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><p>A patron of the arts, he bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Flemish paintings as governor, and his return in 1715 marked the beginning of Bavarian Rococo. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphenburg-palace">Nymphenburg Palace</a> was enlarged, the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleissheim-palace">Schleissheim Palace</a> was completed in 1726, and Joseph Effner built the hunting lodge of Fürstenried. His son Charles Albert later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maximilian II Emanuel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Max Emanuel</b> allied with France in the War of the Spanish Succession and lost his lands after Blenheim in 1704, and Charles Albert was elected Emperor Charles VII in 1742 during the Austrian Succession war.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1703" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maximilian II Emanuel, Elector of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1703">What was here in 1703? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
